@page "/otlp"

<PageTitle>OTLP - bit Belemetry</PageTitle>

<h1>OTLP</h1>

<p class="page-intro">
    OTLP/HTTP is OpenTelemetry's own wire protocol, and the one exporter that reaches almost every backend: the
    OpenTelemetry Collector, the .NET Aspire dashboard, Grafana, Jaeger, Honeycomb, Datadog, New Relic, Elastic and
    more. Belemetry writes it by hand - protobuf and JSON, without the OpenTelemetry packages - from both the .NET
    pipeline and the browser script, with one configuration.
</p>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Adding the exporter</h2>
    <p>
        <code>AddOtlpExporter</code> takes the base endpoint, or a callback for everything else, or both. Any number of
        exporters can be added; each record goes to all of them. The browser script exports with the same settings
        unless <code>IncludeInBrowser</code> is off.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@BasicCode" Language="C#" Expanded="true" />
    <div class="callout">
        This site exports to itself: the WebAssembly app and the browser script post OTLP/JSON to an in-process
        receiver at <code>/otlp</code>, which is what the <a href="/live">Live telemetry</a> page and the feeds on
        every page read from. JSON was chosen so every payload is readable in the browser's network tab.
    </div>
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Protobuf or JSON</h2>
    <p>
        <code>Protocol</code> picks the encoding. <code>HttpProtobuf</code> (the default,
        <code>application/x-protobuf</code>) is the most widely supported and the smallest on the wire.
        <code>HttpJson</code> (<code>application/json</code>) is the OTLP JSON mapping - ids as hex, 64-bit integers as
        strings, enums as numbers - and is easier to inspect and to receive without generated code. gRPC is not
        supported: it needs HTTP/2 trailers, which browsers cannot send.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@ProtocolCode" Language="C#" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Endpoints</h2>
    <p>
        <code>Endpoint</code> is the base URL; <code>/v1/traces</code>, <code>/v1/logs</code> and
        <code>/v1/metrics</code> are appended to it, exactly as <code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT</code> works.
        <code>TracesEndpoint</code>, <code>LogsEndpoint</code> and <code>MetricsEndpoint</code> are full URLs, used as
        they are - for backends whose routes do not follow the convention. Without any endpoint, the exporter posts to
        <code>http://localhost:4318</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>BrowserEndpoint</code> is the base URL the browser script uses instead of <code>Endpoint</code>: a public,
        CORS-enabled route to the collector while the server talks to it over an internal address. The per-signal
        endpoints apply to the browser only when it shares <code>Endpoint</code>. <code>Signals</code> restricts what
        is sent - a traces-only backend such as Jaeger gets <code>BelemetrySignals.Traces</code>.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@EndpointsCode" Language="C#" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Headers, compression and timeout</h2>
    <p>
        <code>Headers</code> are added to every request - typically the vendor's API key.
        <code>Compression = BelemetryCompression.Gzip</code> sends <code>Content-Encoding: gzip</code> (in the browser
        through <code>CompressionStream</code>, except for the final batch sent while the page unloads, which cannot
        wait for it). <code>Timeout</code> bounds one export request (10 s, the OTLP default). Failed requests are
        retried with backoff, honoring <code>Retry-After</code>, and kept for later with the
        <a href="/persistence">offline persistence</a>.
    </p>
    <div class="callout warn">
        Headers the browser script uses are visible to anyone who opens the page. Never put a credential in an
        exporter the browser exports to: set <code>IncludeInBrowser = false</code> on it and give the browser a
        credential-free route (a collector in front of the vendor), or use the
        <a href="/transport">Relay transport</a>, which hands browser records to .NET. Relay only hides the key when
        .NET runs outside the browser - Blazor Server or Hybrid. In WebAssembly the .NET pipeline is itself in the
        browser, and so is its configuration.
    </div>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@HeadersCode" Language="C#" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Environment variables</h2>
    <p>
        Outside the browser, with <code>options.UseEnvironmentVariables</code> (on by default), the standard OTLP
        variables are read - which is how the Aspire AppHost and most container platforms wire telemetry into an app
        without code changes. <code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT</code> alone adds an OTLP exporter when none is
        configured, and fills <code>Endpoint</code> on those that have none. Headers from the variable are added
        unless the code set the same name. A protocol of <code>http/protobuf</code> or <code>http/json</code> sets
        <code>Protocol</code>; any other value (<code>grpc</code>) is ignored.
    </p>
    <div class="doc-table-wrap">
        <table class="doc-table">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Variable</th><th scope="col">Effect</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>OTEL_SERVICE_NAME</code></td><td><code>service.name</code>, when <code>ServiceName</code> is not set</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES</code></td><td>Extra resource attributes (<code>k=v,k2=v2</code>)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT</code></td><td>Adds an OTLP exporter when none is configured, and fills unset endpoints</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS</code></td><td>Request headers (<code>k=v,k2=v2</code>)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL</code></td><td><code>http/protobuf</code> or <code>http/json</code>, unless <code>Protocol</code> is set in code</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@EnvCode" Language="Shell" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>.NET Aspire dashboard</h2>
    <p>
        The dashboard is an OTLP receiver with a UI - the quickest way to see traces, logs and metrics locally. Use its
        OTLP/HTTP endpoint with protobuf. The browser posts to it from your app's origin, so allow that origin with
        <code>DASHBOARD__OTLP__CORS__ALLOWEDORIGINS</code> (and <code>DASHBOARD__OTLP__CORS__ALLOWEDHEADERS</code> if
        you send headers). If the dashboard requires an OTLP API key, the browser would have to carry it - keep the
        browser on the Relay transport or on an unsecured local endpoint.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@AspireShellCode" Language="Shell" />
    <CodeSnippet Code="@AspireCode" Language="C#" />
    <p>
        Under an Aspire AppHost, the AppHost sets <code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT</code> for the projects it runs.
        Belemetry speaks OTLP/HTTP only, so make sure that endpoint is the dashboard's HTTP one, or set
        <code>Endpoint</code> explicitly.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>OpenTelemetry Collector</h2>
    <p>
        The Collector is the usual production setup: the app sends to a collector you control, and the collector holds
        the vendor credentials, batches, samples and fans out. The browser posts to it cross-origin, so the http
        receiver needs a <code>cors</code> section; <code>traceparent</code> does not need to be allowed there - it is
        sent to your API, not to the collector.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@CollectorYaml" Language="YAML" Expanded="true" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Grafana</h2>
    <p>
        Grafana Alloy is a Collector distribution: its <code>otelcol.receiver.otlp</code> component takes the same
        http endpoint and <code>cors</code> block, then forwards to Tempo, Loki and Mimir. Grafana Cloud also exposes an
        OTLP gateway directly; it authenticates with Basic auth (instance id and access token), which is a credential -
        so the browser should go through Alloy or a collector rather than to the gateway itself.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@AlloyCode" Language="Alloy" />
    <CodeSnippet Code="@GrafanaCode" Language="C#" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Jaeger</h2>
    <p>
        Jaeger receives OTLP natively on port 4318 - traces only, so restrict the exporter to
        <code>BelemetrySignals.Traces</code> or its logs and metrics requests are rejected. For the browser, allow the
        app's origin: Jaeger v2 is built on the Collector and takes the same <code>cors</code> block as above; Jaeger
        v1 uses <code>--collector.otlp.http.cors.allowed-origins</code>.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@JaegerCode" Language="C#" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Honeycomb</h2>
    <p>
        Honeycomb accepts OTLP/HTTP at <code>https://api.honeycomb.io</code> (<code>https://api.eu1.honeycomb.io</code>
        in the EU) with the API key in the <code>x-honeycomb-team</code> header; metrics also need
        <code>x-honeycomb-dataset</code>. That key is a credential: keep this exporter out of the browser and route
        browser telemetry through a collector, or use the Relay transport on Blazor Server and Hybrid.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@HoneycombCode" Language="C#" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>API</h2>
    <ApiTable TypeName="BelemetryOtlpExporterOptions" />
    <ApiTable TypeName="BelemetryOtlpProtocol" />
</div>

@code {
    private const string BasicCode = """
        builder.Services.AddBitBelemetryServices(options =>
        {
            options.ServiceName = "MyApp";

            // The base endpoint: /v1/traces, /v1/logs and /v1/metrics are appended.
            options.AddOtlpExporter("http://localhost:4318");

            // Or everything at once.
            options.AddOtlpExporter(otlp =>
            {
                otlp.Endpoint = new Uri("https://otel.example.com");
                otlp.Protocol = BelemetryOtlpProtocol.HttpProtobuf;
                otlp.Compression = BelemetryCompression.Gzip;
            });
        });
        """;

    private const string ProtocolCode = """
        options.AddOtlpExporter("http://localhost:4318", otlp =>
        {
            otlp.Protocol = BelemetryOtlpProtocol.HttpJson;     // default: HttpProtobuf
        });
        """;

    private const string EndpointsCode = """
        options.AddOtlpExporter(otlp =>
        {
            // The .NET side talks to the collector over the internal network...
            otlp.Endpoint = new Uri("http://otel-collector:4318");

            // ...and the browser through a public, CORS-enabled route.
            otlp.BrowserEndpoint = new Uri("https://telemetry.example.com");
        });

        // A backend with non-standard routes: full URLs, used verbatim.
        options.AddOtlpExporter(otlp =>
        {
            otlp.TracesEndpoint = new Uri("https://ingest.example.com/otlp/traces");
            otlp.LogsEndpoint = new Uri("https://ingest.example.com/otlp/logs");
            otlp.Signals = BelemetrySignals.Traces | BelemetrySignals.Logs;
        });
        """;

    private const string HeadersCode = """
        options.AddOtlpExporter("https://otlp.vendor.example", otlp =>
        {
            otlp.Headers["x-api-key"] = builder.Configuration["Telemetry:ApiKey"]!;
            otlp.Compression = BelemetryCompression.Gzip;
            otlp.Timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);

            // The key must not reach the page: browser telemetry takes the Relay transport
            // (Blazor Server, Hybrid) or a credential-free collector route instead.
            otlp.IncludeInBrowser = false;
        });
        """;

    private const string EnvCode = """
        OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=checkout
        OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES=service.namespace=shop,deployment.environment.name=staging
        OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://otel-collector:4318
        OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS=x-api-key=abc123
        OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL=http/protobuf
        """;

    private const string AspireShellCode = """
        docker run --rm -it -p 18888:18888 -p 4318:18890 \
          -e DASHBOARD__OTLP__CORS__ALLOWEDORIGINS=https://localhost:5001 \
          -e "DASHBOARD__OTLP__CORS__ALLOWEDHEADERS=*" \
          mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspire-dashboard
        """;

    private const string AspireCode = """
        builder.Services.AddBitBelemetryServices(options =>
        {
            options.ServiceName = "MyApp";
            options.AddOtlpExporter("http://localhost:4318");     // the dashboard's OTLP/HTTP endpoint
        });
        """;

    private const string CollectorYaml = """
        receivers:
          otlp:
            protocols:
              http:
                endpoint: 0.0.0.0:4318
                cors:
                  allowed_origins: ["https://myapp.example.com"]
                  allowed_headers: ["*"]

        processors:
          batch: {}

        exporters:
          otlphttp:
            endpoint: https://otlp.vendor.example
            headers:
              x-api-key: ${env:VENDOR_API_KEY}

        service:
          pipelines:
            traces:  { receivers: [otlp], processors: [batch], exporters: [otlphttp] }
            logs:    { receivers: [otlp], processors: [batch], exporters: [otlphttp] }
            metrics: { receivers: [otlp], processors: [batch], exporters: [otlphttp] }
        """;

    private const string AlloyCode = """
        otelcol.receiver.otlp "default" {
          http {
            endpoint = "0.0.0.0:4318"
            cors {
              allowed_origins = ["https://myapp.example.com"]
              allowed_headers = ["*"]
            }
          }

          output {
            traces  = [otelcol.exporter.otlphttp.grafana_cloud.input]
            logs    = [otelcol.exporter.otlphttp.grafana_cloud.input]
            metrics = [otelcol.exporter.otlphttp.grafana_cloud.input]
          }
        }
        """;

    private const string GrafanaCode = """
        // Both sides send to Alloy, which holds the Grafana Cloud credential, so no header here
        // can leak through the page.
        options.AddOtlpExporter(otlp =>
        {
            otlp.Endpoint = new Uri("http://alloy:4318");                  // internal, for .NET
            otlp.BrowserEndpoint = new Uri("https://alloy.example.com");   // public, CORS-enabled
        });
        """;

    private const string JaegerCode = """
        options.AddOtlpExporter("http://localhost:4318", otlp =>
        {
            otlp.Signals = BelemetrySignals.Traces;     // Jaeger stores traces only
        });
        """;

    private const string HoneycombCode = """
        options.AddOtlpExporter("https://api.honeycomb.io", otlp =>
        {
            otlp.Headers["x-honeycomb-team"] = builder.Configuration["Honeycomb:ApiKey"]!;
            otlp.Headers["x-honeycomb-dataset"] = "myapp-metrics";    // used for metrics only
            otlp.IncludeInBrowser = false;                            // the key must not reach the page
        });

        // Blazor Server / Hybrid: browser records travel to .NET and leave with the key from there.
        options.Browser.Transport = BelemetryBrowserTransport.Relay;
        """;
}
